# 11. Flash vs JavaScript

This is an interesting part of Web history.

During the 2000s, you could roughly think of:

### JavaScript

```text
HTML
 +
CSS
 +
JavaScript
```

and:

### Flash

```text
HTML
 +
Flash Player
 +
ActionScript
```

Flash was often much better for:

* animation
* multimedia
* games
* sophisticated graphics

while JavaScript was closely integrated with:

* HTML
* DOM
* browser APIs

But JavaScript gradually became dramatically more powerful.

---

# 12. Flash vs Java Applet

You previously asked about Java's role in Internet-era programming, so this comparison is particularly useful.

### Java Applet

```text
Browser
   ↓
Java Plugin
   ↓
JVM
   ↓
Java Applet
```

### Flash

```text
Browser
   ↓
Flash Plugin
   ↓
Flash Player
   ↓
SWF / ActionScript
```

Both attempted something similar:

> **Put a portable application runtime inside the Web browser.**

But Flash became much more successful for mainstream Web multimedia.

---

# 13. Flash vs Silverlight

Microsoft later introduced **Silverlight**.

The rough competition was:

| Platform    | Company          | Language/runtime            |
| ----------- | ---------------- | --------------------------- |
| Flash       | Macromedia/Adobe | ActionScript / Flash Player |
| Silverlight | Microsoft        | .NET / CLR                  |
| Java Applet | Sun              | Java / JVM                  |
| JavaScript  | Web ecosystem    | Browser JS engine           |

These technologies competed to provide **rich applications inside browsers**.

Eventually, however, the Web platform itself became powerful enough that plugins were no longer necessary.

---

# 14. Why did Flash disappear?

This is the key historical question.

Several things happened.

### ① HTML5 became powerful

Modern HTML introduced native capabilities such as:

```text
<video>
<audio>
<canvas>
<svg>
```

So things that previously required Flash could increasingly be done directly by browsers.

For example:

```text
Old Web:

Video
 ↓
Flash
 ↓
Flash Player
```

became:

```text
Modern Web:

<video>
 ↓
Browser
```

---

### ② JavaScript became much faster

JavaScript engines improved enormously.

For example:

```text
Old JavaScript
     ↓
interpreted / relatively slow
```

became:

```text
Modern JavaScript
     ↓
JIT compilation
     ↓
high performance
```

Technologies such as V8 helped make JavaScript a serious application-development platform.

---

### ③ Browsers gained powerful APIs

Modern browsers now provide APIs for:

* graphics
* audio
* video
* networking
* storage
* workers
* WebSockets
* WebGL
* WebAssembly
* device access

So the browser itself became the runtime.

This is a huge architectural transition:

```text
1990s/2000s

Browser
   ↓
Plugin
   ↓
Application


Modern Web

Browser
   ↓
Web platform
   ↓
Application
```

---

### ④ Security problems

Flash had a long history of security vulnerabilities.

Because Flash Player was installed on millions of computers and could execute complex code, it became an attractive attack surface.

The plugin model itself became increasingly unpopular.

---

### ⑤ Mobile devices

Flash also struggled with the rise of smartphones.

Apple famously did not support Flash on iPhone/iPad.

Steve Jobs published the famous **"Thoughts on Flash"** argument in 2010.

The mobile Web increasingly moved toward:

```text
HTML5
CSS
JavaScript
```

instead of Flash.

---

# 15. The end of Flash

Adobe announced the end of Flash development in **2017**.

Flash Player reached its official end of life on **December 31, 2020**.

Major browsers subsequently removed or disabled Flash support.

So:

```text
1996
FutureSplash / Flash begins
       ↓
2000s
Flash dominates Web multimedia
       ↓
2005
Adobe acquires Macromedia
       ↓
2010s
HTML5 + JavaScript become dominant
       ↓
2017
Adobe announces Flash EOL
       ↓
2020
Flash Player officially ends
```

---

# 16. What replaced Flash?

There wasn't one single replacement.

Instead, **the Web platform absorbed many of Flash's capabilities**.

| Flash capability     | Modern Web technology       |
| -------------------- | --------------------------- |
| Animation            | CSS / JavaScript / SVG      |
| Vector graphics      | SVG                         |
| 2D graphics          | Canvas                      |
| Video                | HTML5 `<video>`             |
| Audio                | HTML5 `<audio>` / Web Audio |
| Web applications     | JavaScript / TypeScript     |
| Browser games        | Canvas / WebGL / WebGPU     |
| 3D graphics          | WebGL / WebGPU              |
| Client-side code     | JavaScript / TypeScript     |
| Portable binary code | WebAssembly                 |

This is one of the most important developments in Web history.

